<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Canisius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1521">1521</a>–<a href="/years/1597">1597</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Petrus Canisius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter Canisius was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> priest, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a>, who became one of the leading figures of the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> in the German-speaking lands. His catechisms were reprinted hundreds of times, and he founded or guided Jesuit colleges across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He spent his last years in Fribourg, where he founded the College of Saint Michael in 1582; he was canonised and declared a Doctor of the Church in 1925.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-canisius.jpg" alt="Peter Canisius" width="320" height="513" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Petrus_Canisius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peter Canisius, in Dutch Pieter Kanis, was born in 1521 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a> in the Duchy of Guelders, the son of a wealthy burgomaster, Jacob Kanis. His mother, Ægidia van Houweningen, died shortly after his birth. He studied at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and took a master's degree in 1540, at the age of 19. There he met Peter Faber, one of the founders of the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, and in 1543 he became the first Dutchman to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>. He was ordained in 1546.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Canisius attended several sessions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> in 1547 and became an influential preacher and teacher in the German-speaking lands. He supervised the founding and upkeep of the first German-speaking Jesuit colleges, often with few resources. In 1554 he was offered the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> but declined, serving only as administrator of the diocese for a year. He was one of the main Catholic theologians at the Colloquy of Worms in 1557 and main preacher in the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> from 1559 to 1568. In 1562 he founded what became the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>.</p><p>His lasting contribution is three catechisms, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and German, which defined the basic principles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in the German language and spread widely in Catholic regions. He urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to be more flexible with German Protestants, arguing that an honest explanation of the faith would be more effective than a polemical attack, and he rejected attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a>. He also wrote on the Virgin Mary, in a book of 1577 ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>For the last twenty years of his life Canisius lived in Fribourg, where he founded the Jesuit Collège Saint-Michel. In 1591, at the age of 70, he suffered a stroke that left him partly paralysed, but he went on preaching and writing with the help of a secretary until his death in Fribourg on 21 December 1597.</p><p>He was beatified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> in 1864 and canonised and declared a Doctor of the Church by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-xi">Pope Pius XI</a> on 21 May 1925. From the mid-19th century German churchmen, among them Michael Cardinal von Faulhaber, regarded him as a new Apostle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, a successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>. Schools and colleges in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a>, Buffalo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter Canisius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It remained Catholic during the Reformation and became a centre of the Counter-Reformation: Saint <b>Peter Canisius</b> founded the Jesuit college of Saint Michael there in 1582.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-fribourg">Canton of Fribourg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in Fribourg, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.85,5.86&amp;year=1570" aria-label="Nijmegen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4682 371 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter Canisius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5132 641h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5132 641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.85,5.86&amp;year=1570">What was here in 1570? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
